      "content": "Research on wealth taxes in countries like Germany and Sweden shows that while some high-net-worth individuals move assets, the overall tax base remains stable due to strong inventory and compliance mechanisms. Capital flight appears to be a temporary reaction rather than a permanent life-or-death shift for most investors, provided the tax doesn't rise too quickly or become excessively burdensome. Consequently, data indicates the long-term benefit of reduced inequality usually outweighs the short-term friction of avoiding small asset adjustments.",
